      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? gollum's favorite leaf? i don't get that. and what do you mean about goblin's revenge? i just want to know how to fix the pH levels and what kind of fertilizer i should actually buy. can you give me specific steps for the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Forgive me, I got a bit carried away with the goblin references. The \"gollum's favorite leaf\" was just a metaphor for the lower leaves being drained of nutrients. Think of it like a goblin's favorite snack - they'll eat whatever they can get their hands on, but they'll leave the choicest bits for themselves.\n\nNow, about the pH levels and fertilizer. To fix the pH, you'll need to test the soil and adjust it accordingly. You can buy a soil testing kit or send a sample to a lab for analysis. If the pH is too high, you can add elemental sulfur or peat moss to lower it. If it's too low, you can add lime.\n\nFor fertilizer, you'll want to choose a balanced one that contains nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium. A good all-purpose fertilizer is a 10-10-10 NPK formula. You can also consider a fertilizer specifically formulated for tomatoes, which may contain additional micronutrients like magnesium and calcium.\n\nHere's a step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Test the soil pH and adjust it if necessary.\n2. Choose a balanced fertilizer (10-10-10 NPK) or a tomato-specific fertilizer.\n3.",
